1
00:04:56,470 --> 00:05:02,470
Finishing the Game: The Search for a New Bruce Lee is
a comedy film directed by Justin Lin.

2
00:09:52,941 --> 00:09:58,941
In 1973, martial arts great Bruce Lee died, his final
film, Game of Death, left unfinished.

3
00:14:49,411 --> 00:14:55,411
With the public hungry for more Lee, movie execs decide to find a replacement.

4
00:19:45,882 --> 00:19:51,882
This outrageous satire looks at the entire process, from the oddball candidates to
the greed and racial motivations that drive the final decision.

5
00:24:42,352 --> 00:24:48,352
There's big business in the movies, and Finishing the Game
skewers it with an eye for '70s detail.

6
00:29:38,823 --> 00:29:44,823
Breeze Loo, played by Roger Fan.

7
00:34:35,294 --> 00:34:41,294
Tarrick Tyler, played by McCaleb Burnett.

8
00:39:31,764 --> 00:39:37,764
Cole Kim, played by Sung Kang.

9
00:44:28,235 --> 00:44:34,235
Troy Poon, played by Dustin Nguyễn.

10
00:49:24,705 --> 00:49:30,705
Raja, played by Mousa Kraish.

11
00:54:21,176 --> 00:54:27,176
Dean Silo, played by James Franco.

12
00:59:17,647 --> 00:59:23,647
Finishing the Game is a 2007 mockumentary film directed by Justin Lin focusing on Bruce Lee's
final movie Game of Death (1972), which was unfinished at the time of his death.

13
01:04:14,117 --> 01:04:20,117
Shot in 18 days, Finishing the Game comically satirizes the 1972 production—which used body doubles and
clips from other Lee movies—and addresses racial stereotypes in the Asian community.

14
01:09:10,588 --> 01:09:16,588
Its world premiere took place at the 2007 Sundance Film
Festival, where it was an Official Selection.

15
01:14:07,058 --> 01:14:13,058
It was also selected as the opening night film at the 25th San
Francisco International Asian American Film Festival.

16
01:19:03,529 --> 01:19:09,529
The tone is lighthearted.
